* whetstones have been kicked around for awhile and i favor the simpler
implementation along these lines (followup to other posts):

a whetstones' utility is restoration not enchantment. whetstones appear
as stones but are heavier and have a limited lifetime (i.e. can be worn
away). successfully using a whetstone requires uninterrupted work (i.e.
each application requires N number of uninterrupted moves to be successful).
whetstones affect bladed weapons and less effectively they may be
applied to metal armor (i.e. requires a longer term to provide 1
increment of restoration). whetstones can enchant up to the limit of +1
(i.e. given enough time can upgrade a -3 long sword to +1) and then
have no effect (if you're lucky). if N is the number of moves required
to restore a bladed weapon by 1 increment a whetstone should have an
approximate lifetime of 10N. an uncursed whetstone applied to an axe
could conceivably upgrade the axe to an axe named Cleaver.

